In article <6154.2669770a@uwovax.uwo.ca> 7103_2622@uwovax.uwo.ca (Eric Smith) writes:
>As for the question of whether there should be a confirm for pools of
>water/floating eyes/really big nasty monsters or whatever, it seems that
>people support this for one of two reasons: (1) they have problems with
>line noise or slow machine response, or (2) they're too lazy to look where
>they're going. I don't know how the rest of the development team feels,
>but frankly I think the users in category (2) get what they deserve.

Granted - if it's just laziness, we really don't have any valid reason to
change the game.  But it's not!
Let me give an analogy to illustrate how I, for one, feel.  I think this
is in line with what others have been saying about strategy vs. skill in
Nethack.
Suppose someone said, "The only reason people use functions is that
they're too lazy to produce and maintain n separate copies of the routine."
Or "The only reason people use high-level languages is that they're too
lazy to write a good assembly-language program for each target machine."
Ha.  These statements are absurd.  Why spend my time and attention on
these details that can be taken care of mechanically, in a quick and
reliable way?  I love playing nethack.  The strategy intrigues me.
Like, if I'm in a corridor between two titans, with nothing but an
unidentified wand, and 21 hp's.  That's fun.  Long-term strategies are
fun, too.  But carefully stepping along, looking out for obvious deathtraps
is not fun; if I were to be that careful, I would find it very tedious
and eyestraining.  That is a job for a machine.

>...
>All this will have to wait for 3.1. In the meantime, if you really don't like
>something about the game, you've got the source code; go ahead and "fix"
>it.

Good point.  I'm sure some of us will.  Naturally, though, there's an
advantage to making it official; i.e. future patches to our source code
will work.

